--TEST--
`is` answers for a `Core` class the way it answers for a declared one, and a `Core` class is final for the test — `rule:types/class-reference-sites`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Box {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// A `mixed` is where a `Core` instance arrives — decoded back from an isolate's
// answer, handed over by `Core\Debug`, returned by a member that declares one —
// and naming its class is how a program gets it back.
mixed $date = Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 17);
mixed $box = new Box();
var $uri = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.test/a?b=c");

// The true edge narrows, which is the whole point of asking: a `mixed` that
// answers is a `Core\Time\Date` after the test, with its members reachable and
// no `as` to throw (`rule:types/narrowing`).
if ($date is Core\Time\Date) {
    echo $date->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "|\n";
}
if ($uri is Core\Uri) {
    echo "a uri|\n";
}

// The test reads the value's own descriptor, so a `Core` class answers for
// itself and for nothing else: there is no hierarchy under one to walk, and a
// declared class and a `Core` class never answer for each other.
if (!($box is Core\Time\Date)) {
    echo "a box is no date|\n";
}
if (!($date is Core\Time\DateTime)) {
    echo "a date is no datetime|\n";
}
if (!($date is Box)) {
    echo "a date is no box|\n";
}
if (!($box is Core\Uri)) {
    echo "a box is no uri|\n";
}
--EXPECT--
2026-09-17|
a uri|
a box is no date|
a date is no datetime|
a date is no box|
a box is no uri|
